  "post": {
    "caption": "The Ballon d'Or is brass inside. Mellerio, a Paris jewelry house, has made every Ballon d'Or since 1956. Under a thin layer of gold, the trophy is brass. Two brass halves are welded together to form the ball, which is filled with a mixture called cement so the metal can be pushed out to show the seams. An engraver then draws the seams by hand. The ball sits on a base of natural pyrite. At the end the whole trophy is gilded in gold, and the winner's name is engraved at the last moment. Making one starts six months before delivery and takes almost 100 hours.",
